## Windows preview SDK

The downloadable SDK targets Windows x64 and includes its app-local runtime,
native host, checker, CLI, layout preview and VS Code extension 0.1.7. The selected
Windows 10 LTSC evaluation VM ran without a global .NET SDK or system C++ runtime.
Fresh isolated VS Code 1.145.2 profiles passed two editor processes: checking and
recovery, source enrollment, native launch/stop/restart, diagnostics and reopening.
A fresh SDK-created app also displayed a responsive window, ran a scheduled
callback and exited successfully. This is a virtual test profile with earlier
test installations, not a virgin OS or a physical-input qualification.

## Linux and other targets

Windows Release and Linux/GTK each pass all 43 native suites. Windows and Linux
each pass all 59 editor integration tests without skips. These Linux automated
checks do not establish a fresh Linux SDK or real Linux VS Code recipient. The
published binary bundle is Windows x64.

GTK performance development remains separate. Browser and mobile hosts retain
their existing experimental profiles; this SDK release does not expand their
device support.

## Still open

Fresh Windows 11, real Windows input methods, physical accessibility/usability,
ordinary downloaded-file trust, signing/publisher authenticity and a fresh Linux
editor recipient remain open. NumericInput's headless owner-input tests do not
qualify physical IME or assistive technology. Do not infer support for an unlisted
device from desktop or headless results.

The SDK is experimental and unsigned. Checksums establish unchanged bytes, not
publisher authenticity. Keep SDKs side by side for pinned projects.
